WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
In the spring of 1983, Chrysler began advertising its new Dodge Caravan as a garageable alternative

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.180
to the station wagon.

00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:13.520
The vehicle was built on a truck chassis that qualified for the lighter federal fuel

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:14.060
standard.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:19.820
Within a decade, the same regulatory distinction had turned the minivan and its taller cousin

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:24.080
the SUV into the dominant family vehicles on American roads.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:29.960
The law had been written to reduce oil consumption after the 1973 embargo.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:36.320
Instead, it helped shift the light vehicle fleet toward larger, thirstier models, whose

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:43.160
sales share rose from under 20% in the early 1980s to more than half by the late 1990s.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:51.580
In 1973 and 1974, the Arab oil embargo had cut US supplies by roughly 5%.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:57.060
Gasoline prices jumped from $0.36 to $0.54 a gallon in a single year.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:04.380
In December 1975, President Gerald Ford signed the Energy Policy and Conservation Act.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:10.360
The act created corporate average fuel economy standards administered by the National Highway

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:12.000
Traffic Safety Administration.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:19.560
Lawmakers set an initial target of 27.5 miles per gallon for passenger cars by model year

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:20.360
1985.

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:27.920
They left light trucks defined at the time, mainly as pickups and cargo vans on a slower schedule

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.300
that began around 17.5 miles per gallon.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:37.340
The distinction reflected the view that commercial vehicles served different economic purposes

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:39.960
and could not be redesigned as quickly.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:47.060
At the time, fewer than one in five light vehicles sold was a truck, so the loophole appeared

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:48.340
modest and manageable.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:53.660
The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration published the first car standard

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:58.780
of 18 miles per gallon for 1978 and raised it in annual steps.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:04.840
Manufacturers responded by downsizing engines, improving transmissions and shedding weight.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:15.100
Average car fuel economy climbed from 13.5 miles per gallon in 1975 to 28.5 miles per gallon by 1988.

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:21.700
Light truck standards rose more slowly, reaching 20.7 miles per gallon by 1996.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:26.880
Early compliance reports showed the car fleet meeting or exceeding targets.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:32.480
Both the Carter and Reagan administrations praised the program for cutting projected oil demand.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:38.880
A handful of analysts inside the Department of Transportation noted that the truck definition

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:42.120
rested on vehicle attributes rather than actual use.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:45.780
The observation did not alter the regulatory text.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:51.500
Automakers quickly realized that any vehicle classified as a light truck could be built to a

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:56.180
lower fuel economy target and still count toward the company's overall average.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:01.360
In 1983, Chrysler certified the caravan and voyager as trucks.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:07.040
General Motors and Ford followed with the Chevrolet Astro and Ford AeroStar.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:12.220
By the late 1980s, the same regulatory category was being used for

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:17.900
four-door sport utility vehicles whose passenger compartments were marketed directly to families.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:22.880
Because each additional truck sold relaxed the pressure on the car fleet,

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:29.280
manufacturers invested engineering resources in truck platforms and advertising budgets in truck

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:36.820
imagery. Between 1980 and 2000, the share of light trucks in total US sales rose from 18%

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:41.820
to 52%. The net effect on fleet-wide fuel economy was measurable.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:49.160
After rising steadily through the 1980s, average miles per gallon for all new light vehicles flattened

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:55.360
and then declined slightly in the 1990s, even as individual car models became more efficient.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:02.240
Second order effects compounded the shift. Larger heavier SUVs changed road design standards,

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:07.360
increased the height of headlight beams and altered the mix of vehicles in fatal crashes.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:12.840
By the time the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration began studying the safety

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:19.540
consequences in the late 1990s, more than 40% of new vehicle registrations were light trucks.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:26.160
Congress attempted to close the gap in the 2007 Energy Independence and Security Act.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:32.300
The act directed the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration to set attribute-based

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:38.240
standards that varied with vehicle footprint. The change reduced the incentive to enlarge a vehicle

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:43.880
simply to qualify as a truck. The Obama Administration later tightened the rules further,

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:53.000
projecting a 54.5 miles per gallon fleet average by 2025. Those targets were rolled back in 2020

00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:00.800
and then reinstated in 2024. Each change produced new compliance modeling rather than a single stable

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:07.080
rule. Meanwhile, the installed base of SUVs and crossovers continues to dominate sales.

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:13.400
The average curb weight of new vehicles has increased roughly 1,000 pounds since 1980.

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:19.940
No subsequent policy has reversed the physical transformation of the US fleet that the original

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:26.140
car truck distinction helped set in motion. Regulatory distinctions based on fixed categories

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:32.200
invite reclassification rather than genuine redesign. When the cost of meeting a standard

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:37.700
differs sharply across categories, market actors will migrate volume toward the lower cost

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:44.480
category, even if that migration undercuts the original goal. The corporate average fuel economy

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:51.020
experience suggests that durable policy requires either uniform standards across substitutes or

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:57.320
continuous adjustment mechanisms that anticipate redefinition. The same principle appears whenever

00:05:57.320 --> 00:06:02.780
rules attempt to separate good from bad versions of economically interchangeable products.

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:09.720
Electric vehicle credits, building energy codes and emissions trading categories all face similar

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:15.800
pressures. How might today's emerging rules for autonomous or electric vehicles embed similar

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:21.260
category boundaries that future markets will simply walk around? That wraps today's case.

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:27.360
